          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.307,
            0.849,
            0.371
          ],
          "content": "2. Draw a quadrilateral whose diagonals have equal lengths of 8 cm that bisect each other, and intersect at an angle of (i) $3 0 ^ { \\circ }$ (ii) $4 0 ^ { \\circ }$ (iii) $9 0 ^ { \\circ }$ (iv) $1 4 0 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.379,
            0.848,
            0.438
          ],
          "content": "3. Consider a circle with centre O. Line segments PL and AM are two perpendicular diameters of the circle. What is the figure APML? Reason and/or experiment to figure this out."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.446,
            0.801,
            0.504
          ],
          "content": "4. We have seen how to get $9 0 ^ { \\circ }$ using paper folding. Now, suppose we do not have any paper but two sticks of equal length, and a thread. How do we make an exact $9 0 ^ { \\circ }$ using these?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.817,
            0.44,
            0.905,
            0.494
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.513,
            0.819,
            0.591
          ],
          "content": "5. We saw that one of the properties of a rectangle is that its opposite sides are parallel. Can this be chosen as a definition of a rectangle? In other words, is every quadrilateral that has opposite sides parallel and equal, a rectangle?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.826,
            0.508,
            0.929,
            0.601
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.61,
            0.548,
            0.635
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4.2 Angles in a Quadrilateral"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.646,
            0.847,
            0.686
          ],
          "content": "？ Is it possible to construct a quadrilateral with three angles equal to $9 0 ^ { \\circ }$ and the fourth angle not equal to $9 0 ^ { \\circ } ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.689,
            0.847,
            0.729
          ],
          "content": "You might have observed through constructions that this may not be possible."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.737,
            0.29,
            0.76
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ But why not?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.763,
            0.847,
            0.8
          ],
          "content": "This is due to a general property of quadrilaterals related to their angles."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.799,
            0.848,
            0.839
          ],
          "content": "We have seen that the sum of the angles of a triangle is 180°. There is a similar regularity in the sum of the angles of a quadrilateral."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.084,
            0.915,
            0.12,
            0.936
          ],
          "content": "94"
        },
